For the same total number of training tokens, increasing context length \(L\to 4L\) changes the number of sequences by \(1/4\), while the quadratic attention cost per sequence grows by \(16\times\). Thus total attention FLOPs grow by \(4\times\), not \(16\times\). Linear/projection and FFN costs are essentially unchanged per token.

Using the usual training-FLOP estimate for a vanilla transformer,
\[
C(L)\approx 6N + 12\,n_{\text{layer}}L\,d_{\text{attn}},
\]
where
\[
N \approx n_{\text{layer}}\left(4d_{\text{model}}d_{\text{attn}}+2d_{\text{model}}d_{\text{ff}}\right),
\]
the additional cost from \(L\to4L\) is
\[
\Delta C = C(4L)-C(L)=36\,n_{\text{layer}}L\,d_{\text{attn}}.
\]
So the additional cost as a ratio of the original total cost is
\[
\frac{\Delta C}{C(L)}
=
\frac{36\,n_{\text{layer}}L\,d_{\text{attn}}}
{6N+12\,n_{\text{layer}}L\,d_{\text{attn}}}
=
\frac{3L\,d_{\text{attn}}}
{2d_{\text{model}}d_{\text{attn}}
+d_{\text{model}}d_{\text{ff}}
+L\,d_{\text{attn}}}.
\]

If the standard case \(d_{\text{attn}}=d_{\text{model}}\) is assumed, this simplifies to
\[
\boxed{
\frac{\Delta C}{C(L)}
=
\frac{3L}{2d_{\text{model}}+d_{\text{ff}}+L}
}
\]
and the new total cost ratio is
\[
\boxed{
\frac{C(4L)}{C(L)}
=
1+\frac{3L}{2d_{\text{model}}+d_{\text{ff}}+L}
=
\frac{2d_{\text{model}}+d_{\text{ff}}+4L}
{2d_{\text{model}}+d_{\text{ff}}+L}
}.
\]

Equivalently: the extra compute is \(3\times\) the original attention compute. If attention is a fraction \(f\) of total compute, the extra cost is \(\approx 3f\) of the original total, and the new total is \(\approx (1+3f)\times\) the original. In the attention-dominated limit this approaches \(4\times\) total compute; if attention is negligible, the increase is small.

Let \(d_{\rm attn}\) be the **total** attention width across heads. Ignoring constants, biases, LayerNorms, and nonlinearities, the per-token training FLOPs for a vanilla transformer layer scale as

\[
C(L)\;\propto\; d_{\rm model}d_{\rm ff}+2d_{\rm model}d_{\rm attn}+L\,d_{\rm attn}.
\]

The first two terms are feedforward and attention projections, which do not depend on context length. The last term is the \(O(L)\) per-token attention-score/value cost; per sequence it is \(O(L^2)\), but with the same total number of tokens, the number of sequences drops by \(4\), so the total attention cost increases by \(4\times\), not \(16\times\).

Thus for the same total token budget,

\[
\boxed{
R=\frac{C(4L)}{C(L)}
=
\frac{d_{\rm model}d_{\rm ff}+2d_{\rm model}d_{\rm attn}+4L\,d_{\rm attn}}
{d_{\rm model}d_{\rm ff}+2d_{\rm model}d_{\rm attn}+L\,d_{\rm attn}}
}
\]

and the **additional** cost relative to the original is

\[
\boxed{
R-1
=
\frac{3L\,d_{\rm attn}}
{d_{\rm model}d_{\rm ff}+2d_{\rm model}d_{\rm attn}+L\,d_{\rm attn}}
}
\]

If \(d_{\rm attn}\) is instead the per-head dimension, replace \(d_{\rm attn}\) by \(n_{\rm head}d_{\rm attn}\):

\[
R=
\frac{d_{\rm model}d_{\rm ff}+2d_{\rm model}n_{\rm head}d_{\rm attn}+4L n_{\rm head}d_{\rm attn}}
{d_{\rm model}d_{\rm ff}+2d_{\rm model}n_{\rm head}d_{\rm attn}+L n_{\rm head}d_{\rm attn}}.
\]

So the “best estimate” is not simply \(4\times\) unless attention dominates. If attention dominates, total compute is \(\approx 4\times\) original, i.e. about \(3\times\) **additional** compute. If FFN/projection costs dominate, the increase is much smaller.

For the same total number of training tokens, increasing context length \(L\to 4L\) scales only the attention score/value computation; the feedforward and projection costs per token stay roughly constant.

Using the usual FLOPs estimate, per token per layer:

\[
C(L)\approx 8d_{\text{model}}d_{\text{attn}}
+4d_{\text{model}}d_{\text{ff}}
+4L\,d_{\text{attn}} .
\]

Here \(d_{\text{attn}}\) is the total attention width; if instead it denotes per-head width, replace it by \(n_{\text{head}}d_{\text{attn}}\). The \(n_{\text{layer}}\) factor cancels in the ratio.

Thus the **total training compute ratio** is

\[
\boxed{
R=
\frac{8d_{\text{model}}d_{\text{attn}}
+4d_{\text{model}}d_{\text{ff}}
+16L\,d_{\text{attn}}}
{8d_{\text{model}}d_{\text{attn}}
+4d_{\text{model}}d_{\text{ff}}
+4L\,d_{\text{attn}}}
}
\]

Equivalently,

\[
R = 1+\frac{12L d_{\text{attn}}}
{8d_{\text{model}}d_{\text{attn}}+4d_{\text{model}}d_{\text{ff}}+4L d_{\text{attn}}}.
\]

So:

- If attention is negligible relative to FFN/projections: \(R\approx 1\).
- If attention dominates: \(R\approx 4\).
- The **attention-only** compute increases by \(4\times\), i.e. an additional \(3\times\) original attention compute.
- The overall additional cost over the original is \(R-1\).

For a typical LLM where FFN/projection FLOPs dominate at moderate context lengths, the overall increase is often substantially less than \(4\times\), e.g. roughly \(1.1\)–\(1.4\times\) for many common configurations, depending on \(d_{\text{model}},d_{\text{ff}},d_{\text{attn}}\), and \(L\).

Using the standard training-FLOP estimate, the per-token, per-layer cost can be written as

\[
C(L)\approx 12L\,d_{\text{attn}}
+24d_{\text{model}}d_{\text{attn}}
+12d_{\text{model}}d_{\text{ff}},
\]

where \(L\) appears only in the attention-score / attention-value part. Here \(n_{\text{head}}\) does not appear separately if \(d_{\text{attn}}=n_{\text{head}}d_{\text{head}}\) is the total attention width.

Increasing the context from \(L\) to \(4L\), while keeping the total number of training tokens fixed, gives total-compute ratio

\[
R=
\frac{4L\,d_{\text{attn}}
+2d_{\text{model}}d_{\text{attn}}
+d_{\text{model}}d_{\text{ff}}}
{L\,d_{\text{attn}}
+2d_{\text{model}}d_{\text{attn}}
+d_{\text{model}}d_{\text{ff}}}.
\]

Equivalently, the additional compute as a fraction of the original is

\[
R-1=
\frac{3L\,d_{\text{attn}}}
{Ld_{\text{attn}}
+2d_{\text{model}}d_{\text{attn}}
+d_{\text{model}}d_{\text{ff}}}.
\]

So the attention-matrix part alone becomes about \(4\times\) larger for the same total token count, but the total training-cost ratio is usually less than \(4\times\) because the projection and feedforward terms do not scale with context length. In the limit where attention dominates, the total cost approaches \(4\times\) the original, i.e. about \(3\times\) additional compute. For typical model sizes where feedforward/projection costs are substantial, the increase is much smaller.

Using the acid dissociation equilibrium:

\[
K_a=\frac{[\mathrm{H_3O^+}][\mathrm{HCO_3^-}]}{[\mathrm{CO_2}]}
\]

so

\[
\frac{[\mathrm{HCO_3^-}]}{[\mathrm{CO_2}]}
=\frac{K_a}{[\mathrm{H_3O^+}]}
\]

At pH \(=7.40\),

\[
[\mathrm{H_3O^+}]=10^{-7.40}=3.98\times10^{-8}\ \mathrm{M}
\]

\[
\frac{[\mathrm{HCO_3^-}]}{[\mathrm{CO_2}]}
=\frac{4.3\times10^{-7}}{3.98\times10^{-8}}
\approx 10.8
\]

\[
\boxed{\frac{[\mathrm{HCO_3^-}]}{[\mathrm{CO_2}]}\approx 11:1}
\]

So bicarbonate concentration is about \(10.8\) times the carbon dioxide concentration, or approximately \(11:1\).

For the equilibrium

\[
\mathrm{CO_2 + 2H_2O \rightleftharpoons H_3O^+ + HCO_3^-}
\]

the acid dissociation constant is

\[
K_a=\frac{[\mathrm{H_3O^+}][\mathrm{HCO_3^-}]}{[\mathrm{CO_2}]}
=4.3\times 10^{-7}
\]

At pH \(=7.40\),

\[
[\mathrm{H_3O^+}]=10^{-7.40}=3.98\times 10^{-8}\ \mathrm{M}
\]

Thus,

\[
\frac{[\mathrm{HCO_3^-}]}{[\mathrm{CO_2}]}
=
\frac{K_a}{[\mathrm{H_3O^+}]}
=
\frac{4.3\times10^{-7}}{3.98\times10^{-8}}
\approx 10.8
\]

\[
\boxed{\frac{[\mathrm{HCO_3^-}]}{[\mathrm{CO_2}]}\approx 10.8 \approx 11}
\]

So bicarbonate is about **10.8 times more concentrated than dissolved carbon dioxide**, i.e. roughly a **11:1** ratio.

