For a vanilla transformer, when you keep the **total number of training tokens fixed**, increasing the context length from \(L\) to \(4L\) does **not** multiply attention compute by \(4^2=16\). Per sequence attention cost grows as \(L^2\), but there are \(1/4\) as many sequences for the same token budget, so the total attention contribution grows by about \(4\times\).

Assuming \(d_{\rm attn}\) is the total attention/projection width, per-token training FLOPs are proportional to

\[
C(S)\;\propto\;
8\,d_{\rm model}d_{\rm attn}
+4\,d_{\rm model}d_{\rm ff}
+4S\,d_{\rm attn},
\]

where \(S\) is the context length. Thus

\[
\frac{C(4L)}{C(L)}
=
\frac{
8 d_{\rm model}d_{\rm attn}
+4 d_{\rm model}d_{\rm ff}
+16L d_{\rm attn}}
{
8 d_{\rm model}d_{\rm attn}
+4 d_{\rm model}d_{\rm ff}
+4L d_{\rm attn}}
\]

or simplified,

\[
\boxed{
\frac{C(4L)}{C(L)}
=
\frac{
2 d_{\rm model}d_{\rm attn}
+d_{\rm model}d_{\rm ff}
+4L d_{\rm attn}}
{
2 d_{\rm model}d_{\rm attn}
+d_{\rm model}d_{\rm ff}
+L d_{\rm attn}}
}
\]

So the **additional** compute relative to the original is this ratio minus 1:

\[
\boxed{
\frac{\Delta C}{C(L)}
=
\frac{
3L d_{\rm attn}}
{
2 d_{\rm model}d_{\rm attn}
+d_{\rm model}d_{\rm ff}
+L d_{\rm attn}}
}
\]

If \(d_{\rm attn}=d_{\rm model}\), this becomes

\[
\boxed{
\frac{C(4L)}{C(L)}
=
\frac{2d_{\rm model}+d_{\rm ff}+4L}
{2d_{\rm model}+d_{\rm ff}+L}
}
\]

and the additional fraction is

\[
\boxed{
\frac{\Delta C}{C(L)}
=
\frac{3L}
{2d_{\rm model}+d_{\rm ff}+L}
}.
\]

The exact ratio depends on the model dimensions and on \(L\). If attention dominates, it approaches \(4\times\); if FFN/projection costs dominate, it stays closer to \(1\times\). The number of heads \(n_{\rm head}\) cancels if \(d_{\rm attn}\) is the total attention width.

The context-length increase does **not** multiply total training compute by \(16\). For the same total number of tokens, only the quadratic attention part grows; the projections and FFN stay roughly constant per token.

Let  
\[
D_{\text{attn}}=
\begin{cases}
n_{\text{head}}\,d_{\text{attn}}, & \text{if }d_{\text{attn}}\text{ is per head},\\
d_{\text{attn}}, & \text{if }d_{\text{attn}}\text{ is already the total attention width}.
\end{cases}
\]

Ignoring constants from softmax, norms, biases, etc., the per-token per-layer cost is approximately
\[
C_{\text{tok}}(L)\propto 4d_{\text{model}}D_{\text{attn}}
+2d_{\text{model}}d_{\text{ff}}
+2LD_{\text{attn}}.
\]
Therefore
\[
\boxed{
\frac{C(4L)}{C(L)}
=
\frac{2d_{\text{model}}D_{\text{attn}}
+d_{\text{model}}d_{\text{ff}}
+4LD_{\text{attn}}}
{2d_{\text{model}}D_{\text{attn}}
+d_{\text{model}}d_{\text{ff}}
+LD_{\text{attn}}}
}
\]
for the **new total compute**, relative to the original.

If by “additional computational cost” you mean the extra amount beyond the original,
\[
\boxed{
\frac{C(4L)-C(L)}{C(L)}
=
\frac{3LD_{\text{attn}}}
{2d_{\text{model}}D_{\text{attn}}
+d_{\text{model}}d_{\text{ff}}
+LD_{\text{attn}}}
}
\]

So the total cost ratio is between \(1\) and \(4\). It is close to \(1\) when FFN/projection costs dominate, and approaches \(4\) when attention costs dominate. The extra cost over the original approaches \(3\times\) in the attention-dominated regime.

For steady one-dimensional radial conduction through the pipe and insulation:

\[
q'=\frac{\Delta T}{R'_{\text{pipe}}+R'_{\text{ins}}}
\]

where

\[
R'_{\text{cylinder}}=\frac{\ln(r_o/r_i)}{2\pi k}
\]

Radii:

\[
r_i=3\text{ in}=0.25\text{ ft}
\]
\[
r_o=3.25\text{ in}=0.27083\text{ ft}
\]
\[
r_{\text{ins,out}}=3.25+3.5=6.75\text{ in}=0.5625\text{ ft}
\]

Pipe resistance:

\[
R'_{\text{pipe}}
=\frac{\ln(0.27083/0.25)}{2\pi(26)}
=4.90\times10^{-4}\ \frac{\text{hr}\cdot\text{ft}\cdot^\circ\text{F}}{\text{Btu}}
\]

Insulation resistance:

\[
R'_{\text{ins}}
=\frac{\ln(0.5625/0.27083)}{2\pi(0.04)}
=2.9081\ \frac{\text{hr}\cdot\text{ft}\cdot^\circ\text{F}}{\text{Btu}}
\]

Total resistance:

\[
R'_{\text{total}}=2.9086\ \frac{\text{hr}\cdot\text{ft}\cdot^\circ\text{F}}{\text{Btu}}
\]

Heat loss per foot:

\[
q'=\frac{500-150}{2.9086}
\approx 120.3\ \text{Btu/(hr}\cdot\text{ft)}
\]

Interface temperature:

\[
T_{\text{interface}}
=500-q'R'_{\text{pipe}}
=500-(120.3)(4.90\times10^{-4})
\approx 499.94^\circ\text{F}
\]

\[
\boxed{q' \approx 1.20\times10^2\ \text{Btu/(hr}\cdot\text{ft)}
\approx 120\ \text{Btu/hr per ft of pipe}}
\]

\[
\boxed{T_{\text{interface}} \approx 499.9^\circ\text{F} \approx 500^\circ\text{F}}
\]

The insulation provides almost all of the thermal resistance, so the pipe/insulation interface remains very close to the pipe inner-surface temperature.
